# Home Assistant Configuration

This is a Home Assistant configuration directory.

## Environment

- **Working directory**: `/homeassistant` — the same directory HA itself
  sees as `/config`. Use `/config/...` inside anything HA executes
  (`shell_command`, `python_script`), and `/homeassistant/...` for your
  own tool calls.
- **URL**: http://homeassistant.local:8123
- Do not hardcode version numbers anywhere. Read them when needed:
  `ha core info` (Core version, update availability), `ha os info`
  (HAOS version).

## Directory Structure

- `configuration.yaml` - Main configuration file
- `automations.yaml` - Automation definitions
- `scripts.yaml` - Script definitions
- `scenes.yaml` - Scene definitions
- `secrets.yaml` - Secret values (API keys, passwords) - DO NOT commit or share
- `customize.yaml` - Entity customizations
- `.storage/` - Internal HA storage (do not edit manually)

## CLI Tools

All `ha-*` tools (`ha-entities`, `ha-dashboard`, `ha-trace`, `ha-history`,
`ha-template`, `ha-service`, `ha-state`, `ha-reload`) are installed on
`PATH`. Invoke
them by bare name exactly as shown below — never `./ha-entities` or any
other path form. A path form is silently rewritten to the bare name before
it runs, so it is never what you meant to type. If a tool call errors, fix
the call instead of falling back to ad-hoc WebSocket scripts via `ha_lib`.

Editing a `.yaml` file under `/homeassistant` triggers an automatic parse
check (syntax plus duplicate top-level keys). If it fails, the error comes
back to you as a tool error — fix it before reloading.

## Discover, Never Guess

Entities, areas, services, and integrations change without this file
changing. **Never write an entity ID, service field, area name, or
integration name from memory or from this file — look it up first.**
The skills are the source of truth:

| Question | Use |
|---|---|
| What is the entity ID for X? What's its state/attributes? | `HAEntities` |
| What areas / domains / scripts / automations exist? | `HAEntities` (`areas`, `domains`, `scripts`, `automations`) |
| What fields does this service take? What are the valid values? | `CallService` — check `ha-service show` before writing service data anywhere |
| Does this Jinja template work? What does it evaluate to? | `TestTemplate` |
| What happened in the past? How long / how often / when last? | `QueryHistory` |
| Why didn't this automation fire, or what did it actually do? | `DebugAutomation` (traces, not YAML guesswork) |
| Setting helper/virtual state (booleans, selects, numbers, timers, counters) | `SetState` (`ha-state`) — validated and verified; raw service calls on these domains are refused |
| Anything touching dashboards, views, cards, badges | `EditDashboard` — **never** edit `.storage/lovelace.*` by hand |
| Applying YAML changes | `HomeAssistantReload` (`ha-reload`) — prefers hot reload over restart |

Useful starting points: `ha-entities list --search "<fragment>"` to find
an entity, `ha-entities areas` for the area list, `ha-entities scripts`
to resolve numeric script IDs to their aliases.

## Common Tasks

### Reload YAML Configuration (PREFERRED)
```bash
ha-reload
```
Use this for YAML changes. It's instant and has no downtime. Validate first
with `yamllint` (and `ha core check` for configuration.yaml changes).
Reloads all hot-reloadable YAML domains (see the HomeAssistantReload skill).

### Full Restart (only when needed)
```bash
ha core restart
```
Only use for: new integrations, logger/recorder/http changes.

### Validate Configuration
```bash
ha core check
```

### View Logs
```bash
ha core logs
```

### Validate YAML Syntax
```bash
yamllint configuration.yaml
```

### Find Entity IDs
```bash
ha-entities list --search "office light"
```
Query entities, areas, domains, scripts, and automations with `ha-entities`
(see the HAEntities skill). Never guess entity IDs or read `.storage/` for them.

### Edit Dashboards
```bash
ha-dashboard list
```
Manage Lovelace dashboards with `ha-dashboard` (see the EditDashboard skill).
Never edit `.storage/lovelace.*` files directly — they go stale.

### Debug Automations
```bash
ha-trace runs automation.morning_lights
```
Inspect why an automation ran, stopped, or failed using execution traces
(see the DebugAutomation skill). Never guess from the YAML alone.

### Query History
```bash
ha-history changes binary_sensor.front_door --last 24h
```
Answer "when/how long/how much" questions from recorded history and
long-term statistics (see the QueryHistory skill).

### Test Templates
```bash
ha-template '{{ states("sun.sun") }}'
```
Render Jinja templates against live state before putting them into
automations, dashboards, or template sensors (see the TestTemplate skill).
Never write a template into config unverified.

### Call Services
```bash
ha-service show light.turn_on
ha-service call light.turn_on --entity light.office --data brightness=200
```
Discover real service parameters and call services (see the CallService
skill). Never guess service fields — `ha-service show` has the actual spec.

### Set Helper State
```bash
ha-state show input_select.house_mode
ha-state set input_boolean.vacation_mode on
```
Mutate helpers, counters, timers, and vars with `ha-state` (see the
SetState skill). It validates the value, calls the right service, and
verifies the result by reading the state back.

### List Custom Components
```bash
ls custom_components/
grep -h '"name"\|"version"\|"documentation"' custom_components/*/manifest.json
```
Enumerate custom components rather than trusting a list written down
anywhere — each `manifest.json` gives the upstream repo, version, and
`iot_class`.

## Best Practices

- Always validate configuration before restarting
- Use `secrets.yaml` for sensitive values
- Use packages in `packages/` directory for modular configuration
- Back up before major changes: `ha backup create`
- Verify automation behaviour with traces (`DebugAutomation`), not by
  re-reading your own YAML
- Older UI-created scripts may have numeric IDs; map them to aliases with
  `ha-entities scripts`, and prefer descriptive slugs for anything new
- Some `light.*`/`switch.*` entities are UI group helpers aggregating
  others — check with `HAEntities` before assuming an entity maps to one
  physical device
- Multiple radio stacks can coexist (ZHA, Zigbee2MQTT, Thread/Matter);
  the owning integration determines a device's trigger style, so confirm
  which stack owns a device before writing triggers for it
- Devices that are unplugged part of the year report `unavailable` for
  months — check history before treating that as a fault
